<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Privacy manifest. Required for App Store submission because the app calls a
  "required reason" API; upload validation checks for this file statically, so
  its absence fails before a human ever sees the build.

  Placement matters: on macOS the manifest goes in Contents/Resources, not next
  to the executable. app/Makefile copies it there.

  The declarations here are deliberately minimal and each one is true today:

  - UserDefaults (CA92.1) — AppState persists this app's own settings (refresh
    interval, icon mode, launch-at-login, pinned-widget visibility) and reads
    back only what it wrote. That is exactly what CA92.1 covers.

  - No tracking, no tracking domains, no collected data types. The dashboard
    never leaves the machine: the app talks to the dish on the local network and
    to nothing else. The Nominatim reverse geocoder that would have made this
    untrue is compiled out of the bundled helper (see features_apphelper.go),
    and buildDashboard carries no coordinates at all.

  If a place-name feature is ever added back, NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes must
  gain a precise-location entry with a third-party-transfer disclosure, and the
  listing needs a privacy-policy URL. Do not add it quietly.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
